window.AILETMOCK={"n":6,"title":"Mock Test 6","theme":"Amendment power and Article 21","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"Amendment power, basic structure, the Emergency, Article 21, natural resources and the President's powers in constitutional law. Seven more branches of law and contemporary legal GK complete the paper.","minutes":120,"neg":0.25,"sec":["Constitutional Law","Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","International Law and Human Rights","Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK"],"q":[{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Amendment Power","q":"In I.C. Golaknath v. State of Punjab (1967), an eleven-judge bench of the Supreme Court held that Parliament:","o":["cannot amend Part III, since an amendment is 'law' under Article 13","can amend Part III only through a specially elected constituent body","can amend Part III only if half of the States ratify the change","can amend any Part of the Constitution without any kind of limit"],"a":0,"e":"The Court overruled Shankari Prasad (1951) and Sajjan Singh (1965) by a 6:5 majority. The 24th Amendment (1971) and then Kesavananda Bharati (1973) overruled Golaknath, though Kesavananda introduced the basic structure limit."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Basic Structure","q":"In Minerva Mills v. Union of India (1980), the Supreme Court struck down clauses (4) and (5) of Article 368, inserted by the 42nd Amendment, because:","o":["Directive Principles always override the Fundamental Rights in Part III","a limited amending power is itself part of the basic structure","the Ninth Schedule can never be used for placing any law in it","Parliament has no power whatsoever to amend the Constitution at all"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that Parliament cannot, by exercising a limited power, enlarge it into an absolute power. It also held that harmony and balance between Parts III and IV is part of the basic structure."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Emergency","q":"In ADM Jabalpur v. Shivkant Shukla (1976), the majority held that during the Emergency a detainee:","o":["could not seek habeas corpus, as Article 21 stood suspended then","could seek habeas corpus, since Article 21 can never be suspended by any order","could seek habeas corpus, but only before the Supreme Court and not a High Court","could challenge the detention only on the ground of procedural illegality in it"],"a":0,"e":"Justice H.R. Khanna alone dissented, holding that life and liberty cannot be taken away without the authority of law. The Supreme Court in Puttaswamy (2017) described ADM Jabalpur as wrongly decided."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Article 21","q":"In Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978), which arose from the impounding of a passport, the Supreme Court held that the 'procedure established by law' in Article 21 must be:","o":["a procedure that the passport authority fixes without giving any reasons","any procedure that a validly enacted law happens to prescribe in its text","a procedure that is wholly separate from the tests under Articles 14 and 19","just, fair and reasonable, and not arbitrary or fanciful"],"a":3,"e":"The Court read Articles 14, 19 and 21 as interconnected, departing from the narrow view in A.K. Gopalan (1950). The right to travel abroad was held to be part of personal liberty."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Fundamental Rights","q":"In R.C. Cooper v. Union of India (1970), the Bank Nationalisation case, the Supreme Court rejected the A.K. Gopalan view that:","o":["banks may be nationalised only through a constitutional amendment by Parliament","each Fundamental Right is a separate and watertight compartment of its own","Fundamental Rights may be enforced against private persons as well as the State","compensation for acquired property must always equal the full market value"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the effect of State action on the rights of a person must be tested under every relevant Article. It also struck down the Act for failing to provide for the principles of compensation then required by Article 31."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Governors","q":"In Subhash Desai v. Principal Secretary, Governor of Maharashtra (2023), the Constitution Bench held that the Governor's decision to call for a floor test was:","o":["void, and so the Court restored the earlier Government to office at once","valid, since the Governor always acts on his own judgment in such matters","valid, because the Governor may call a floor test whenever he wishes to do so","not justified, as he had no objective material on the majority"],"a":3,"e":"The Court did not restore the Thackeray Government because he had resigned without facing the floor test. It also held that a Governor must act on objective material and not on a party's internal differences alone."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Federalism","q":"The original jurisdiction of the Supreme Court under Article 131 covers disputes:","o":["between two High Courts about their respective territorial jurisdiction","between private persons and the Government over Fundamental Rights","between citizens of different States on constitutional questions of law","between the Union and States, or between States, involving a legal right"],"a":3,"e":"Article 131 jurisdiction is original and exclusive. The dispute must involve a question of law or fact on which a legal right depends, and States have invoked it in challenges such as the Kerala suit on borrowing limits."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Natural Resources","q":"In Property Owners Association v. State of Maharashtra (2024), the nine-judge bench held, by majority, that under Article 39(b):","o":["not every private resource is a 'material resource of the community'","the Article is not a Directive Principle and can never be applied at all","the Article applies only to resources that the State itself already owns","every privately owned resource is a material resource of the community"],"a":0,"e":"The majority, led by Chief Justice Chandrachud, held that a private resource may qualify only after a contextual test, such as its nature, scarcity and impact on the community. It disagreed with the expansive view of Justice Krishna Iyer in the Sanjeev Coke line of cases."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Trade and Commerce","q":"Which Article of the Constitution declares that trade, commerce and intercourse throughout the territory of India shall be free?","o":["Article 303","Article 300A","Article 304","Article 301"],"a":3,"e":"Articles 301 to 307 in Part XIII deal with trade within India. In Atiabari Tea Co. (1961) and the Automobile Transport case (1962), the Supreme Court explained that compensatory taxes do not violate this freedom."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Parliament","q":"The President nominates persons with special knowledge or practical experience in literature, science, art and social service to the Rajya Sabha. The maximum number of such members is:","o":["14","10","16","12"],"a":3,"e":"Under Article 80, the Rajya Sabha has a maximum strength of 250 members, of whom 238 represent States and Union Territories and 12 are nominated by the President."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Parliament","q":"Which of the following Bills cannot be resolved through a joint sitting of the two Houses under Article 108?","o":["An ordinary Bill whose amendments the other House refuses to accept","A Constitution Amendment Bill passed by both Houses","An ordinary Bill rejected by the Rajya Sabha after passage by Lok Sabha","An ordinary Bill kept pending by the Rajya Sabha for over six months"],"a":1,"e":"A joint sitting is available only for ordinary Bills, and a Money Bill cannot face a deadlock since the Rajya Sabha has only recommendatory power over it. Joint sittings have taken place in 1961 (Dowry Prohibition Bill), 1978 and 2002 (POTA)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"President","q":"Under Article 61, a resolution to impeach the President for violation of the Constitution must be passed by:","o":["a majority of the total membership of the Lok Sabha acting alone","two-thirds of the total membership of each of the Houses","a simple majority of members present and voting in each of the Houses","two-thirds of members present and voting in the Lok Sabha alone"],"a":1,"e":"The notice must be signed by at least one-fourth of the members of the House that moves the charge and given 14 days in advance. The other House investigates the charge, and the President may appear or be represented."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Inter-State Disputes","q":"Article 262 empowers Parliament to provide by law for the adjudication of disputes relating to:","o":["the trade of goods and services between two or more of the States","the demarcation of boundaries between two or more of the States","the sharing of Union tax revenues between the Union and the States","the use, distribution or control of waters of inter-State rivers"],"a":3,"e":"Parliament enacted the Inter-State River Water Disputes Act, 1956 under this Article, and may bar the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court over such disputes. The Cauvery and Krishna tribunals were constituted under it."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Constitutional Offices","q":"The 102nd Constitution Amendment Act, 2018 inserted Article 338B and granted constitutional status to:","o":["the National Commission for Women in India","the National Commission for Backward Classes","the National Human Rights Commission of India","the National Commission for Minorities in India"],"a":1,"e":"The amendment also inserted Articles 342A and 366(26C). The 105th Amendment (2021) later restored the power of States to identify their own socially and educationally backward classes."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Treaties and India","q":"Article 253 empowers Parliament to make laws for implementing international treaties:","o":["only if the Rajya Sabha first passes a resolution under Article 249","only after half of the State Legislatures have ratified the treaty","on any matter, even one that is otherwise in the State List","only on the matters listed in the Union List of the Seventh Schedule"],"a":2,"e":"The treaty-making power is executive, but giving effect to a treaty within India needs legislation. The Court in Maganbhai Ishwarbhai Patel v. Union (1969) held that a treaty changing Indian territory needs a constitutional amendment."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Federalism","q":"The residuary power of legislation, covering matters in none of the three Lists, is vested under Article 248 in:","o":["the President, on the advice of the Union Council of Ministers","the Inter-State Council on the Governors' recommendation","Parliament, including taxes not mentioned in any of the Lists","the State Legislatures, for matters not in any List"],"a":2,"e":"Article 248 read with Entry 97 of the Union List gives Parliament this power. This is a Union-centric feature of Indian federalism, unlike the United States, where residuary power rests with the States."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Emergency","q":"A Proclamation of Financial Emergency under Article 360 must be approved by both Houses of Parliament within:","o":["two months","one month","six months","three months"],"a":0,"e":"A Proclamation under Article 352 must be approved within one month, and under Article 356 within two months. A Financial Emergency has never been proclaimed in India."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Electoral Law","q":"In Lily Thomas v. Union of India (2013), the Supreme Court struck down Section 8(4) of the Representation of the People Act, 1951, which:","o":["barred persons under investigation from contesting elections in any case","let a convicted legislator keep his seat pending appeal for three months","let candidates contest elections from jail without filing a nomination","let disqualified legislators contest again immediately after disqualification"],"a":1,"e":"As a result, a legislator convicted of an offence with a sentence of two years or more stands disqualified from the date of conviction. A stay on the conviction, and not only on the sentence, is needed to avoid the disqualification."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Free Speech","q":"Romesh Thappar v. State of Madras (1950), which struck down a ban on the circulation of a journal, led to the First Amendment of 1951 that added to Article 19(2) the ground of:","o":["contempt of court","public order","morality and decency","sovereignty and integrity"],"a":1,"e":"The First Amendment also added 'friendly relations with foreign States' and 'incitement to an offence'. 'Sovereignty and integrity of India' was added later by the 16th Amendment (1963)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Reservation","q":"The decision in State of Madras v. Champakam Dorairajan (1951), which struck down a communal order on seat allocation, led the First Amendment to insert:","o":["Article 21A","Article 15(5)","Article 15(4)","Article 16(4A)"],"a":2,"e":"The Court had held that Directive Principles must conform to the Fundamental Rights, which are supreme. Article 15(4) permits special provisions for socially and educationally backward classes and the SCs and STs."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Religious Freedom","q":"In Bijoe Emmanuel v. State of Kerala (1986), the Supreme Court held that expelling students who stood respectfully for the national anthem but did not sing it:","o":["was invalid only because the students had not been heard before expulsion","violated their freedom of conscience, as none can be forced to sing","was valid, since the school had a rule of discipline on all of its students","was valid, because singing is a Fundamental Duty enforceable by the State"],"a":1,"e":"The students, who were Jehovah's Witnesses, held that their religious faith allowed them to honour only Jehovah. The Court relied on Articles 19(1)(a) and 25 and held that the Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act did not cover non-singing."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Rajya Sabha","q":"In Kuldip Nayar v. Union of India (2006), the Supreme Court upheld the amendment removing the requirement of ordinary residence in the State for contesting Rajya Sabha elections, holding that:","o":["the amendment was invalid since it was not ratified by half of the States","the domicile requirement is not part of the basic structure of the Constitution","the domicile requirement is a basic feature, so its removal was unconstitutional","the amendment was valid only for elections held after the next Census"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the right to be elected is a statutory right and the Rajya Sabha's federal character is not defeated by a residence rule. It also upheld open ballot for Rajya Sabha elections."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Free Speech","q":"In S. Rangarajan v. P. Jagjivan Ram (1989), the Supreme Court held that a certified film cannot be suppressed because of:","o":["its being produced by a person who is not a citizen of this country","the pendency of a review by the Central Board of Film Certification","its popularity among a large section of the viewing public at large","the 'heckler's veto', or threats of protest by those opposing it"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that the danger to public interest must be real and proximate, like 'a spark in a powder keg', and the State must protect the freedom against intolerant groups."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Untouchability","q":"Article 17 abolishes untouchability and, unlike most Fundamental Rights, is enforceable against:","o":["private individuals as well as the State itself","only the State and its authorities, not private persons","only public servants acting in the discharge of duty","only the Union Government acting through departments"],"a":0,"e":"Articles 15(2), 17, 23 and 24 operate against private persons too. Parliament enacted the Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1955 under Article 35, and PUDR v. Union of India (1982) confirmed the horizontal operation of Article 23."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under Section 103(2) of the BNS, when a group of five or more persons acting in concert kills a person on grounds such as race, caste or community, each member is punishable with:","o":["imprisonment for life only, without any fine","imprisonment of not less than seven years, and fine","imprisonment up to seven years, and fine","death or imprisonment for life, and fine"],"a":3,"e":"This is the new offence of mob lynching, which had no separate provision in the IPC. Murder by an individual is punishable under Section 103(1) with death or imprisonment for life and fine."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Organised crime, a new offence in Section 111 of the BNS, is punishable, where it results in the death of any person, with:","o":["death or life imprisonment, and fine of at least ₹10 lakh","imprisonment of not less than seven years, and fine of ₹2 lakh","imprisonment of not less than five years, and fine of ₹5 lakh","imprisonment up to ten years, and fine of not less than ₹1 lakh"],"a":0,"e":"Organised crime includes kidnapping, robbery, vehicle theft, extortion, land grabbing, contract killing, economic offences and cyber-crimes committed by a syndicate. Petty organised crime is dealt with in Section 112."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Which of the following is an element in the definition of a 'terrorist act' under Section 113 of the BNS?","o":["taking part in a riot that involves more than ten persons","intent to threaten the unity, integrity or security of India","criminal trespass on a Government building without permission","causing hurt to a public servant who is performing his duty"],"a":1,"e":"Section 113 imports the definition of a terrorist act from the UAPA. A terrorist act resulting in death is punishable with death or imprisonment for life, and fine."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Snatching, treated as a distinct offence in Section 304 of the BNS, is punishable with imprisonment of up to:","o":["seven years, and fine","three years, and fine","five years, and fine","one year, and fine"],"a":1,"e":"Snatching means suddenly or quickly seizing movable property from a person, and is different from theft and robbery. The IPC had no separate offence for it."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Section 69 of the BNS, which punishes sexual intercourse obtained by deceitful means, including a false promise of marriage, provides imprisonment up to:","o":["five years, and fine","seven years, and fine","ten years, and fine","fourteen years, and fine"],"a":2,"e":"The offence is not rape, but a separate offence where consent was obtained by deceit, a promise of employment or promotion, or concealment of identity. Courts distinguish a false promise from a promise broken later."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Section 85 of the BNS, dealing with cruelty by a husband or his relatives towards a married woman, replaces which provision of the IPC?","o":["Section 376","Section 354","Section 498A","Section 304B"],"a":2,"e":"Section 85 is punishable with imprisonment up to three years and fine, and Section 86 defines cruelty. Section 80 of the BNS corresponds to dowry death under Section 304B of the IPC."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under Section 176(3) of the BNSS, a forensic expert must visit the crime scene to collect forensic evidence in offences punishable with imprisonment of:","o":["seven years or more","ten years or more","five years or more","three years or more"],"a":0,"e":"The provision is aimed at improving the quality of investigation, and the State Governments are to notify the facilities within a period of five years."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Section 530 of the BNSS provides that trials, inquiries and other proceedings may be held:","o":["in electronic mode, including examination of witnesses by video link","electronically only before the High Court, and never in a trial court","electronically only in cases of offences punishable with fine alone","only in a physical court, in all cases without any exception to it"],"a":0,"e":"The BNSS also permits summons to be served electronically and recording of statements by audio-video means. The provision promotes the use of technology in criminal procedure."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Section 356 of the BNSS provides for trial in absentia, which allows the court to proceed against:","o":["a convict who has escaped from custody after the judgment is delivered","any accused who refuses to attend the court on a given date of hearing","a proclaimed offender who has absconded, after prescribed safeguards","a witness who has turned hostile during the trial before the court"],"a":2,"e":"The court must first issue two warrants of arrest at an interval of thirty days and publish a notice. The trial may then proceed after ninety days from the framing of charges, and the offender may appeal within three years."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Section 107 of the BNSS, a new provision, empowers a Magistrate, on the application of a police officer, to order:","o":["attachment, forfeiture or restoration of the proceeds of crime","closure of any business premises of a suspect for a year","transfer of a pending case from one court to another court","preventive detention of a suspect for a period of thirty days"],"a":0,"e":"The provision gives a procedure for attaching property derived from crime outside the PMLA framework. The Magistrate has to give notice to the person concerned before forfeiting it."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Which new form of punishment was introduced in Section 4 of the BNS, 2023 for certain petty offences?","o":["community service for petty offences","house arrest as an alternative to jail","public censure through the media","electronic tagging along with parole"],"a":0,"e":"BNS prescribes community service for offences like petty theft, an attempt to compel or restrain a public servant by threatening suicide (Section 226), defamation and appearing in a public place in a state of intoxication causing annoyance. The other punishments are death, imprisonment, forfeiture of property and fine."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BSA","q":"Under Section 63 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023, an electronic record is admissible as documentary evidence if accompanied by:","o":["a sworn affidavit by the maker of the record, and no other document","an order of the High Court permitting production of the electronic record","a hard copy of the record certified by the Registrar of the court","a certificate in the prescribed Schedule, signed by the person in charge and an expert"],"a":3,"e":"The provision replaces Section 65B of the Evidence Act and has a two-part certificate. Arjun Panditrao Khotkar v. Kailash Gorantyal (2020) held that the certificate is mandatory where the original device is not produced."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Provocation","q":"In K.M. Nanavati v. State of Maharashtra (1962), the Supreme Court laid down that the test of 'grave and sudden' provocation is whether:","o":["the accused personally felt provoked at that moment in his own mind","the deceased had earlier been convicted of any offence in a court","the deceased had made a prior threat of any kind to the accused","a reasonable person in his position would have lost self-control"],"a":3,"e":"The Court also required that there be no time for passion to cool between the provocation and the act. The case led to the abolition of jury trials in India. The principle now falls under Exception 1 to Section 101 of the BNS."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Murder","q":"In Virsa Singh v. State of Punjab (1958), the Supreme Court interpreted which limb of the definition of murder, now found in Section 101 of the BNS?","o":["an act done with knowledge that it is so imminently dangerous as to cause death","an act done with the intention of causing the death of the person who is harmed","an injury intended and sufficient in the ordinary course of nature to cause death","an act done in the heat of passion, without premeditation, on grave provocation"],"a":2,"e":"The Court laid down four steps for the prosecution: the existence of a bodily injury, its nature, intention to inflict that very injury, and its sufficiency to cause death in the ordinary course of nature."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Damages","q":"In Kailash Nath Associates v. DDA (2015), the Supreme Court held that under Section 74 of the Contract Act, where a sum is named for breach:","o":["reasonable compensation up to that sum, with loss proved unless impossible to prove","the whole named sum is payable in every case, whatever the actual loss is","no compensation is payable unless the contract has a penalty clause in it","compensation is payable only if the exact loss is proved in money terms"],"a":0,"e":"Section 74 abolishes the distinction between liquidated damages and penalty. Forfeiture of earnest money is covered by Section 74 and must be reasonable."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Restraint of Trade","q":"In Niranjan Shankar Golikari v. Century Spinning (1967), the Supreme Court held that a negative covenant restraining an employee from working elsewhere during the term of employment:","o":["is void under Section 27, being a restraint of trade","is valid only if a High Court has approved it in advance","is valid and not a restraint of trade under Section 27 of the Act","is valid only for a period not exceeding one year from joining"],"a":2,"e":"The Court distinguished restraints operating during the term of the contract from post-termination restraints, which are generally void in India under Section 27."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Indemnity","q":"In Gajanan Moreshwar Parelkar v. Moreshwar Madan Mantri (1942), the Bombay High Court held that the holder of an indemnity:","o":["may sue only after he has paid the loss entirely out of his own pocket","may sue only after the loss has been crystallised by a decree of a court","may sue the indemnifier before actual loss, so as to be put in funds","has no right of suit unless the indemnity is contained in a written deed"],"a":2,"e":"The Court rejected the narrow English rule that the indemnity-holder can sue only after paying, and held that the Indian Contract Act does not restrict the remedy in that way. Section 124 defines the contract of indemnity."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Specific Relief","q":"The 'substituted performance' remedy in Section 20 of the Specific Relief Act, 1963, introduced by the 2018 amendment, allows an aggrieved party, after notice to the defaulter, to:","o":["have the contract performed by an officer of the court at State cost","treat the contract as void and claim nothing beyond the costs of suit","seek an automatic transfer of the entire dispute to an arbitrator","have the contract performed through a third party and recover the cost from the defaulter"],"a":3,"e":"The aggrieved party must give the defaulter a written notice of at least thirty days. If it opts for substituted performance, it cannot claim specific performance of the contract, but may still claim compensation."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Limitation","q":"Under Section 3 of the Limitation Act, 1963, a suit instituted after the prescribed period of limitation must be:","o":["dismissed, even if limitation has not been set up as a defence by him","transferred to a tribunal that has continuing jurisdiction over the dispute","dismissed only if the defendant takes the plea of limitation in defence","stayed until the delay is automatically condoned by the court concerned"],"a":0,"e":"Section 5 permits condonation of delay in appeals and applications but not in suits. Sections 14 and 17 allow exclusion of time spent in bona fide proceedings in a wrong court and in cases of fraud or mistake."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Torts","q":"In Overseas Tankship (UK) v. Morts Dock (Wagon Mound No. 1) (1961), the Privy Council held that damage is recoverable in negligence only if it is:","o":["within the physical area of the first injury caused by the negligence","the direct consequence of the act, however unforeseeable it may have been","of a kind that was reasonably foreseeable by the defendant at the time","of a kind that was actually intended by the defendant in doing the act"],"a":2,"e":"The decision overruled Re Polemis (1921), which had adopted the 'directness' test. The foreseeability test is now the standard test of remoteness in tort."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Torts","q":"Ashby v. White (1703), which established the maxim 'ubi jus ibi remedium', holds that violation of a legal right is actionable:","o":["only when actual pecuniary loss is proved by him","only when the violation is done intentionally","only when the right violated is a right in property","per se, even without proof of actual damage to him"],"a":3,"e":"This is the principle of injuria sine damno. The opposite principle, damnum sine injuria, as in Gloucester Grammar School (1410), holds that loss without violation of a legal right gives no action."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property","q":"Under Section 123 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882, a gift of immovable property must be made by:","o":["an oral declaration made before the Sub-Registrar of the district","delivery of possession alone, without any written document at all","an unregistered deed that is attested by a Magistrate of the area","a registered instrument signed by the donor and attested by two witnesses"],"a":3,"e":"A gift of movable property may be effected by a registered instrument or by delivery. The gift must be accepted during the lifetime of the donor, failing which it is void."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property","q":"Under the doctrine of lis pendens in Section 52 of the Transfer of Property Act, a transfer of the property in suit made during the pendency of the suit:","o":["takes effect only after notice of the transfer is served on the transferee","is void against all parties, whatever the final decision of the court","does not affect the rights of the other party under the court's decree","is valid against all parties, including the court that decides the suit"],"a":2,"e":"The doctrine rests on the maxim 'pendente lite nihil innovetur'. The transfer is not void, but is subject to the outcome of the suit, and the transferee is bound by the decree."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property","q":"Section 53A of the Transfer of Property Act (part performance) is described as 'a shield and not a sword' because a transferee in possession under a written contract may:","o":["force the transferor to get the deed registered in his own favour","claim possession of the property even against the true owner of it","acquire title as full owner of the property against all the world","defend his possession against the transferor, but cannot claim title"],"a":3,"e":"After the 2001 amendment, the transferee can use the doctrine only if he has taken possession and done some act in furtherance of the contract. It does not create title."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Easements","q":"Under Section 15 of the Indian Easements Act, 1882, an easement is acquired by prescription after peaceable, open enjoyment as of right, without interruption, for:","o":["twenty years","fifteen years","thirty years","twelve years"],"a":0,"e":"In the case of an easement over Government property, the period is thirty years. Prescription is a method of acquiring an easement, along with grant and implied grant."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Divorce","q":"In Amardeep Singh v. Harveen Kaur (2017), the Supreme Court held that the six-month waiting period in Section 13B(2) of the Hindu Marriage Act for divorce by mutual consent:","o":["is mandatory, except that a High Court alone may reduce it to three months","is mandatory, and can never be waived by any court in any situation","is not applicable to cases where one party lives outside the country","is directory, and may be waived by the court in appropriate cases"],"a":3,"e":"The Court said the waiver is available where the period of separation is already over, efforts at mediation have failed, and waiting would only prolong the agony of the parties."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Succession","q":"Under Section 15 of the Hindu Succession Act, 1956, property inherited by a childless Hindu woman from her father, if she dies intestate, devolves upon:","o":["her husband alone","the State, by escheat","the heirs of her husband","the heirs of her father"],"a":3,"e":"Section 15(2)(a) provides that property inherited from her father or mother goes, in the absence of a son or daughter, to the heirs of the father. Property inherited from her husband or father-in-law goes to the heirs of the husband."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Justice Theory","q":"Robert Nozick's 'entitlement theory' holds that a distribution of holdings is just if:","o":["it gives every person an equal share of the resources of society","holdings were justly acquired and justly transferred, free of State interference","it maximises the total welfare of the greatest number of persons in society","it works to the greatest benefit of the least advantaged members of society"],"a":1,"e":"Nozick, in 'Anarchy, State, and Utopia', argued for a minimal State and against patterned principles of distribution. He criticised the redistributive approach of John Rawls."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Modern Jurisprudence","q":"Ronald Dworkin used Riggs v. Palmer, where a murderer was denied inheritance under his victim's will, to argue that:","o":["law includes principles, not just rules identified by a pedigree test","judges have unfettered discretion in all the hard cases that come to them","law consists only of rules identified through a rule of recognition","law is the command of a sovereign supported by a threat of a sanction"],"a":0,"e":"Dworkin's attack on legal positivism, particularly on Hart, rested on the role of principles in adjudication. His later idea of 'law as integrity' views judges as writing a chain novel."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Marxist Theory","q":"In Marxist legal theory, law is a part of the 'superstructure' which:","o":["reflects the economic base and serves the ruling class interests","arises from universal moral principles that all human beings share","stands wholly independent of the economic relations in society","rests on the command of a determinate sovereign backed by sanction"],"a":0,"e":"For Marx and Engels, the legal order is shaped by the mode of production. They expected law to wither away with the end of class divisions."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Feminist Jurisprudence","q":"Catharine MacKinnon's feminist jurisprudence argues that the law:","o":["protects the interests of women alone in every area of social life","treats gender as a purely biological fact with no social meaning","is wholly neutral between the sexes in the way that it operates","reflects male power and treats the male view as the neutral standard"],"a":3,"e":"MacKinnon's 'dominance' approach looks at the subordination of women, in contrast to a 'sameness' approach based on formal equality. Her work influenced the law on sexual harassment."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Critical Legal Studies","q":"The Critical Legal Studies movement, associated with Roberto Unger and Duncan Kennedy, argues that legal doctrine is:","o":["grounded in the pure reason of nature, which is discoverable by judges","a closed system of commands that are issued by the sovereign alone","indeterminate and shaped by politics, not a neutral science","determinate, and yields a single right answer for each legal dispute"],"a":2,"e":"The movement, which began in the United States in the 1970s, drew on legal realism and Marxism. It holds that law legitimises existing hierarchies."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'res ipsa loquitur', applied in Byrne v. Boadle (1863), means that:","o":["an act done by an agent binds the principal who authorised it","the thing cannot be proved without the evidence of an eyewitness","the thing lost must be borne by the person who owns that thing","the thing speaks for itself, raising an inference of negligence"],"a":3,"e":"In Byrne v. Boadle, a barrel of flour fell from the defendant's warehouse and hit the plaintiff. The burden shifts to the defendant to explain, where the accident would not ordinarily happen without negligence."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'ex turpi causa non oritur actio' means that:","o":["no action lies where the claimant has not proved actual damage","no action arises from a base or illegal cause of action","no action arises where the claimant has given his consent to it","no action lies once the period of limitation has expired in law"],"a":1,"e":"A claimant cannot ordinarily recover where his claim is founded on his own illegal act, as in Holman v. Johnson (1775). Section 23 of the Indian Contract Act reflects the principle."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Ownership","q":"A.M. Honoré's analysis of the 'incidents of ownership' includes among them the right to:","o":["possess, use, manage, and take the income of the thing","manage alone, but never to enjoy any income from it","use alone, with no right to transfer it by sale, gift or bequest","possess alone, since ownership and possession are one and the same"],"a":0,"e":"Honoré listed eleven incidents, including the right to capital, the right to security, transmissibility, and the duty to prevent harm. Ownership is thus a bundle of rights and not a single right."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Theories of Punishment","q":"The reformative theory of punishment, which regards crime as a disease of the mind, is summed up in the idea that:","o":["an eye must be given for an eye and a tooth for a tooth","punishment must deter other persons from committing crime","the offender must be kept away from society to prevent harm","one should hate the sin and not the sinner"],"a":3,"e":"The theory underlies probation, parole and juvenile justice. Indian courts have referred to it in cases such as Mohd. Giasuddin v. State of Andhra Pradesh (1977)."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Interpretation","q":"The 'literal rule' of interpretation, which says words must be given their natural and ordinary meaning, was articulated in:","o":["Sussex Peerage Case (1844)","Heydon's Case, decided in 1584","Grey v. Pearson, decided in 1857","Pepper v. Hart, decided in 1993"],"a":0,"e":"If the words are clear, the court must apply them even if the result seems harsh. The golden rule modifies it to avoid absurdity, and the mischief rule looks at the defect that the law was meant to remedy."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Interpretation","q":"The 'golden rule' of interpretation, permitting departure from the literal meaning only to avoid absurdity or repugnancy, is associated with:","o":["Lord Coke in Heydon's Case, decided in the year 1584","Lord Diplock in Duport Steels v. Sirs, decided in the year 1980","Lord Wensleydale in Grey v. Pearson (1857)","Lord Denning in Magor and St Mellons v. Newport Corporation (1952)"],"a":2,"e":"The rule modifies the grammatical meaning only so far as is necessary to avoid the absurdity, and no further. It is a modification of the literal rule and not a rejection of it."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Constitutional Interpretation","q":"In Prafulla Kumar Mukherjee v. Bank of Commerce, Khulna (1947), the Privy Council applied the doctrine of pith and substance to:","o":["decide the List to which a law belongs by looking at its true nature","prefer the State List over the Union List in every case of conflict","limit the legislative power of Parliament during a proclaimed Emergency","strike down any law that incidentally affects a subject in another List"],"a":0,"e":"Under the doctrine, incidental encroachment on another List does not make a law invalid if its pith and substance falls within the competence of the enacting legislature. The Supreme Court uses it for federal disputes."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Jurisdiction","q":"In the S.S. Lotus case (France v. Turkey, PCIJ, 1927), the Court held that a State may exercise jurisdiction over acts abroad:","o":["only if the flag State of the vessel has consented to the exercise of it","unless a rule of international law prohibits it from doing so","only if a treaty has expressly permitted it to exercise such jurisdiction","only if the accused person is a national of the State that prosecutes"],"a":1,"e":"The 'Lotus principle' reflects a permissive view of State sovereignty. It was later limited by Article 11 of the 1958 Geneva Convention on the High Seas and Article 97 of UNCLOS on collisions at sea."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Custom","q":"In the Anglo-Norwegian Fisheries case (1951), the ICJ recognised that a State which persistently objects while a customary rule is forming:","o":["is bound after five years if it does not renew its objection in writing","is not bound by that rule, as against that State alone","is bound by it, since custom binds all States of the world equally","is not bound only if it is a permanent member of the Security Council"],"a":1,"e":"This is the 'persistent objector' principle. It does not apply to peremptory norms (jus cogens), which bind all States whatever their objection."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Erga Omnes","q":"In the Barcelona Traction case (Belgium v. Spain, 1970), the ICJ described obligations erga omnes as obligations:","o":["owed to the international community as a whole, so all States have an interest","owed only to those States that are parties to the Statute of the Court","owed only to the State that was injured by a breach of a bilateral treaty","owed only to the State of nationality of the affected shareholders"],"a":0,"e":"The Court gave the examples of the prohibition of aggression and genocide, and the protection from slavery and racial discrimination. The same Court limited the diplomatic protection of shareholders."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Human Rights Institutions","q":"Under the Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993, as amended in 2019, the Chairperson of the National Human Rights Commission is:","o":["only a person who has been the Chief Justice of any High Court of India","a person who has been the Chief Justice of India or a Supreme Court Judge","a person of eminence in human rights, nominated by the Prime Minister","only a person who has been the Chief Justice of India, with no exceptions"],"a":1,"e":"Before 2019, only a former Chief Justice of India was eligible. The Chairperson is appointed by the President on the recommendation of a committee headed by the Prime Minister."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Environmental Law","q":"The 'no-harm' principle, that a State must not allow its territory to be used to cause serious injury to another State, was first stated in:","o":["the Lotus Case (France v. Turkey), of the year 1927","the Island of Palmas Case (Netherlands v. United States)","the Nottebohm Case (Liechtenstein v. Guatemala), of 1955","the Trail Smelter Arbitration (United States v. Canada)"],"a":3,"e":"The tribunal held that Canada was responsible for sulphur fumes from a smelter in British Columbia that damaged farms in Washington State. The principle is reflected in Principle 21 of the Stockholm Declaration."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"United Nations","q":"The 'Uniting for Peace' resolution (Resolution 377 A (V), 1950) allows the UN General Assembly to consider a matter and recommend collective measures where:","o":["the International Court of Justice asks for an urgent advisory opinion","the Security Council fails to act due to a veto in a threat to peace","the Secretary-General alone has certified a threat to world peace","a Member State has withdrawn from the Charter of the United Nations"],"a":1,"e":"The resolution was used in the Korean War and again, for example, in the emergency special sessions on Ukraine. The Assembly's recommendations are not binding."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Trade Law","q":"Since December 2019, the Appellate Body of the World Trade Organization has been unable to hear appeals because of:","o":["its abolition under a Ministerial Declaration that was adopted in 2017","the withdrawal of India from the Dispute Settlement Understanding","a blockage of appointment of new members, leaving it without a quorum","a decision of the General Council to merge it with the panels"],"a":2,"e":"The Appellate Body needs three members to hear an appeal. A group of members set up the Multi-Party Interim Appeal Arbitration Arrangement as a stop-gap. (Verify the current position of the reform talks.)"},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Law of the Sea","q":"In the Corfu Channel case (UK v. Albania, 1949), the ICJ held Albania responsible for explosions that damaged British warships because Albania had:","o":["failed to warn ships of the mines in its waters which it knew about","fired on British warships that were exercising a right of innocent passage","laid the mines itself in the channel, as was proved with direct evidence","closed the strait to international navigation by an armed blockade"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that every State is under an obligation not to knowingly allow its territory to be used for acts contrary to the rights of other States. It also held that the UK's later mine-sweeping operation violated Albanian sovereignty."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Treaty Law","q":"In its 1951 Advisory Opinion on Reservations to the Genocide Convention, the ICJ held that a State making a reservation to which some parties object can still be a party if the reservation is:","o":["approved by the Security Council, with the concurrence of its permanent members","approved unanimously by all of the contracting parties to the Convention","compatible with the object and purpose of the Convention","approved by a resolution of the UN General Assembly after due debate"],"a":2,"e":"The 'compatibility test' was later adopted in Article 19(c) of the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, 1969."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Copyright","q":"The moral rights of an author under Section 57 of the Copyright Act, 1957, that is, the rights of paternity and integrity, continue:","o":["even after the author has assigned the copyright in the work","only until the copyright in the work is assigned to another person","only for literary works, and not for artistic or musical works","only for a period of ten years from the date of the publication"],"a":0,"e":"In Amar Nath Sehgal v. Union of India (2005), the Delhi High Court upheld the artist's rights when the Government stored and damaged his bronze mural, holding that the right survives assignment."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Patents","q":"Under Section 3(k) of the Patents Act, 1970, which of the following is NOT patentable?","o":["a new industrial process producing a novel alloy","a new mechanical device that has an inventive step","a computer programme per se, without any technical effect","a new synthetic chemical compound with a novel use"],"a":2,"e":"Section 3(k) excludes mathematical methods, business methods, computer programmes per se and algorithms. Software with a technical effect can still be patented, as in Ferid Allani v. Union of India (Delhi High Court, 2019)."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Geographical Indications","q":"Which was the first product in India to receive a Geographical Indication tag, in 2004-05?","o":["Basmati rice","Kancheepuram silk","Mysore silk","Darjeeling tea"],"a":3,"e":"The Geographical Indications of Goods (Registration and Protection) Act, 1999 came into force in 2003. The GI Registry is located in Chennai."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Cyber Law","q":"Cheating by personation using a computer resource, such as online impersonation, is punishable under Section 66D of the IT Act, 2000 with imprisonment up to:","o":["seven years, and fine up to ₹10 lakh","one year, and fine up to ₹10,000","three years, and fine up to ₹1 lakh","five years, and fine up to ₹5 lakh"],"a":2,"e":"Section 66C punishes identity theft, with the same punishment of three years and fine up to ₹1 lakh. The BNS now covers cheating by personation under Section 319."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Company Law","q":"Under the 'doctrine of indoor management', established in Royal British Bank v. Turquand (1856), an outsider dealing with a company in good faith:","o":["must verify that every resolution was passed before dealing with it","may assume that the internal formalities were duly complied with","must inspect the company's minute books before every dealing with it","is bound by the internal rules, whether or not they were public"],"a":1,"e":"The doctrine is an exception to the rule of constructive notice. It does not protect a person who has actual notice of an irregularity, or where the document is a forgery (Ruben v. Great Fingall)."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Corporate Governance","q":"Under Section 135 of the Companies Act, 2013, a company covered by the CSR provisions must spend on CSR activities at least:","o":["2 per cent of the net profit of the immediately preceding year only","2 per cent of the average net profit of the preceding three years","5 per cent of the average net profit of the preceding three years","1 per cent of the annual turnover of the preceding financial year"],"a":1,"e":"The provision applies to companies with net worth of ₹500 crore or more, turnover of ₹1,000 crore or more, or net profit of ₹5 crore or more in the preceding financial year."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Corporate Governance","q":"Under Section 149(4) of the Companies Act, 2013, a listed public company must have independent directors forming at least:","o":["two-thirds of the total number of directors","one-fourth of the total number of directors","one-third of the total number of directors","one-half of the total number of directors"],"a":2,"e":"Section 149(6) lays down the criteria for independence. The Act also requires a listed company to have at least one woman director."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Arbitration","q":"In Cox and Kings Ltd. v. SAP India (2023), the Constitution Bench held that the 'group of companies' doctrine:","o":["binds every company in a group solely because of common ownership of shares","can bind a non-signatory to an arbitration agreement, based on intention and conduct","applies only to arbitrations that are seated outside India, and not in India","can never be applied, since only the signatories are bound to arbitrate at all"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the doctrine rests on the mutual intention of the parties, and it also depends on factors such as direct relationship and commonality of subject matter. (Verify the present status of the matter on remand.)"},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Arbitration","q":"Under Section 29A of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996, an arbitral tribunal must make its award within twelve months, which the parties may extend, by consent, by up to a further:","o":["twelve months","six months","nine months","three months"],"a":1,"e":"Any extension beyond this period needs the permission of the court, and the court may reduce the fees of the arbitrators if the delay is attributable to them. International commercial arbitrations are not bound by the twelve-month limit."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Partnership","q":"A Limited Liability Partnership under the LLP Act, 2008 is:","o":["an unincorporated association that is identical to a partnership firm","a company that needs a minimum of seven members to be formed at all","a body that must have a minimum share capital at the time of its formation","a body corporate with a separate legal personality and perpetual succession"],"a":3,"e":"The LLP needs at least two partners, and the liability of a partner is generally limited to his agreed contribution. The LLP agreement governs mutual rights and duties."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Insurance Law","q":"Under Section 45 of the Insurance Act, 1938 (as amended in 2015), an insurer cannot call a life insurance policy in question on grounds of misstatement after:","o":["five years from the date of the policy, except on proof of fraud","ten years from the date of the policy, except on proof of fraud","three years from the date of the policy, except on proof of fraud","one year from the date of the policy, except on proof of fraud"],"a":2,"e":"Even within three years, the insurer must show that the misstatement was material and that it was made fraudulently. The principle of utmost good faith (uberrima fides) governs insurance contracts."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Legitimate Expectation","q":"In Navjyoti Coop. Group Housing Society v. Union of India (1992), the Supreme Court recognised that the doctrine of legitimate expectation:","o":["creates an enforceable right to the expected benefit in every case that arises","applies only where a statute has granted a vested right to the person concerned","protects a person from arbitrary departure from past practice, without a hearing","has no application to any administrative decision taken in the country"],"a":2,"e":"The doctrine is a part of natural justice and the rule of non-arbitrariness. It gives procedural protection, and it may be overridden by public interest."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Promissory Estoppel","q":"In Motilal Padampat Sugar Mills v. State of U.P. (1979), the Supreme Court held that the doctrine of promissory estoppel is:","o":["applicable against the Government, as against a private person","applicable only if there is a formal contract under Article 299","applicable only against private parties, and never against the State","inapplicable to the Government in the exercise of any of its powers"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that the promisee need not suffer detriment, but he must have altered his position in reliance on the promise. The doctrine cannot compel the Government to act against the law or public interest."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Anti-Corruption","q":"The Central Vigilance Commission, which exercises superintendence over the CBI in corruption cases, derives its statutory status from:","o":["Article 324A of the Constitution, as inserted by amendment","the Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act, 2013, which created it","a Cabinet resolution of 1964, with no later Act of Parliament","the Central Vigilance Commission Act, 2003, enacted by Parliament"],"a":3,"e":"The CVC was set up on the Santhanam Committee's recommendation in 1964 and was given statutory status after the directions in Vineet Narain v. Union of India (1998)."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Public Trust Doctrine","q":"In M.C. Mehta v. Kamal Nath (1997), the Supreme Court applied the public trust doctrine to hold that:","o":["natural resources may be freely leased to private persons by the State","the State holds rivers and forests as a trustee for the public","only the Union may hold natural resources, as their absolute owner","the doctrine applies to land alone, and not to water or air at all"],"a":1,"e":"The Court quashed the lease of forest land on the bank of the Beas to a motel. The doctrine, taken from Roman law, restricts the State's power to alienate resources meant for public enjoyment."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Environmental Law","q":"In Vellore Citizens' Welfare Forum v. Union of India (1996), the Supreme Court held that the 'precautionary principle' and the 'polluter pays' principle are:","o":["not part of Indian law until a statute has been enacted to adopt them","part of the law of the country, as essential features of sustainable development","limited to industries situated in coastal areas of the country alone","applicable only in disputes that have an international element in them"],"a":1,"e":"The case concerned tanneries polluting the Palar river in Tamil Nadu. The Court read these principles into Articles 21, 47, 48A and 51A(g), and into the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Biodiversity","q":"The National Biodiversity Authority, set up under the Biological Diversity Act, 2002 to regulate access to biological resources and benefit sharing, is headquartered at:","o":["Dehradun","Hyderabad","Chennai","Bhopal"],"a":2,"e":"The Act implements the Convention on Biological Diversity, 1992. It has a three-tier structure of the NBA, State Biodiversity Boards and Biodiversity Management Committees at the local level."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Whistleblowers","q":"Under the Whistle Blowers Protection Act, 2014, a public interest disclosure about corruption in the Union Government is to be made to:","o":["the Lokpal alone, in every case of a disclosure that is made","the Chief Justice of the concerned High Court of the State","the Competent Authority, which is the Central Vigilance Commission","the Comptroller and Auditor General of India, as its Chairman"],"a":2,"e":"The Act protects the identity of the whistleblower and bars victimisation. For a State Government employee, the Competent Authority is the State Vigilance Commission, and for judges it is the Chief Justice. The Act is yet to be fully operationalised since its rules are awaited."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Reproductive Rights","q":"The Surrogacy (Regulation) Act, 2021 permits surrogacy in India only in the form of:","o":["altruistic surrogacy, while commercial surrogacy is prohibited","commercial surrogacy, regulated by a licence given to the clinic","international surrogacy, which is meant only for foreign couples","both altruistic and commercial surrogacy by registered clinics"],"a":0,"e":"An altruistic surrogate must be a close relative who is a married woman with a child of her own, under the original Act. Selling or buying human embryos and commercial surrogacy are offences."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Reproductive Rights","q":"In X v. Principal Secretary, Health and Family Welfare Department (2022), the Supreme Court held that unmarried women are entitled to a safe abortion up to:","o":["12 weeks, as the law fixes a lower limit","24 weeks, on par with married women under the Act","28 weeks, but only if the husband consents","20 weeks, the limit for married women only"],"a":1,"e":"The Court read the 2021 amendment to the Medical Termination of Pregnancy Act to cover unmarried women, and held that the distinction between married and unmarried women violated Article 14. It also held that marital rape is covered for MTP purposes."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Judiciary","q":"Who succeeded Justice B.R. Gavai as the Chief Justice of India in November 2025?","o":["Justice Vikram Nath","Justice D.Y. Chandrachud","Justice Surya Kant","Justice Sanjiv Khanna"],"a":2,"e":"Justice Surya Kant was sworn in as the 53rd Chief Justice on 24 November 2025. (Verify the tenure end date, which is expected in early 2027.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Elections","q":"The Constitution (129th Amendment) Bill, 2024, introduced in the Lok Sabha in December 2024, relates to:","o":["delimitation of Lok Sabha constituencies after the next Census of the country","the creation of a National Judicial Commission for the appointment of judges","reservation of one-third of the seats in Parliament for women candidates","simultaneous elections to the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies"],"a":3,"e":"The Bill, with the Union Territories Laws (Amendment) Bill, was referred to a Joint Parliamentary Committee. It proposes a new Article 82A. (Verify the present status of the Bills.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Constitutional Developments","q":"The Constitution (130th Amendment) Bill, 2025, introduced in the Lok Sabha in August 2025, proposes the removal of a Prime Minister, Chief Minister or Minister who has been:","o":["convicted by a court of any offence involving moral turpitude","charged under any provision of the PMLA by the Directorate of Enforcement","arrested and detained for thirty consecutive days on a serious charge","declared a defaulter in respect of any loan that was taken from a bank"],"a":2,"e":"The Bill covers offences punishable with imprisonment for five years or more, and was referred to a Joint Parliamentary Committee. Critics cite the presumption of innocence and federalism. (Verify the current status.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Judiciary","q":"Who became the first woman Chief Justice of a High Court in India, in 1991, at the Himachal Pradesh High Court?","o":["Justice Anna Chandy","Justice Ruma Pal","Justice Sujata Manohar","Justice Leila Seth"],"a":3,"e":"Justice Anna Chandy was the first woman High Court judge, in Kerala, in 1959. Justice Leila Seth was also a member of the Law Commission and the mother of the writer Vikram Seth."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Legal Institutions","q":"The Law Commission of India is:","o":["a non-statutory body constituted by a Government resolution","a statutory body that was created by an Act of Parliament in 1955","a body created under Article 39A to advise on the legal aid schemes","a constitutional body that is established under the terms of Article 124A"],"a":0,"e":"The first Law Commission was set up in 1955 under M.C. Setalvad, and the Commission is reconstituted periodically. It makes recommendations on law reform to the Government, which are not binding. (Verify the current Chairperson.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Judiciary","q":"After the Supreme Court (Number of Judges) Amendment Act, 2019, the sanctioned strength of the Supreme Court of India is:","o":["37, including the Chief Justice of India","33, including the Chief Justice of India","34, including the Chief Justice of India","31, including the Chief Justice of India"],"a":2,"e":"The strength was 8 in 1950, and was raised to 11 (1956), 14 (1960), 18 (1977), 26 (1986), 31 (2008) and 34 (2019). Article 124(1) allows Parliament to fix the number."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Treaties and India","q":"India placed the Indus Waters Treaty, 1960 'in abeyance' in April 2025 after an attack in Pahalgam. The Treaty was brokered by:","o":["the Security Council of the United Nations, acting under Chapter VII","the World Bank, which is also a signatory for limited purposes","the Commonwealth Secretariat, which acted as an arbitrator in it","the International Court of Justice, through a decision it delivered"],"a":1,"e":"The Treaty gave India the eastern rivers (Ravi, Beas, Sutlej) and Pakistan the western rivers (Indus, Jhelum, Chenab), with India having limited use of the western rivers. (Verify the latest developments, including the Court of Arbitration proceedings.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Constitution","q":"Constitution Day (Samvidhan Divas), observed on 26 November every year since 2015, marks:","o":["the adoption of the Constitution by the Constituent Assembly in 1949","the commencement of the Constitution of India on 26 January 1950","the first sitting of the Supreme Court of India, which took place in 1950","the first meeting of the Constituent Assembly, held in December 1946"],"a":0,"e":"The Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949 and came into force on 26 January 1950. The day was earlier observed as National Law Day."}],"b":[{"id":"S1","m":25,"w":450,"q":"The Supreme Court has recognised a right against the adverse effects of climate change as part of Articles 14 and 21 (M.K. Ranjitsinh v. Union of India, 2024). Discuss how far environmental jurisprudence in India has shaped State accountability for climate action.","o":["Constitutional basis: Articles 21, 48A and 51A(g); expansion of Article 21 from M.C. Mehta and Subhash Kumar to the right to a healthy environment.","Principles: sustainable development, precautionary principle, polluter pays and public trust (Vellore Citizens, Kamal Nath).","M.K. Ranjitsinh (2024): right against adverse effects of climate change, read with Articles 14 and 21 (Verify the details).","Statutory and institutional tools: Environment (Protection) Act 1986, NGT Act 2010, India's NDCs and net-zero target.","Challenges: enforcement gaps, balancing development, judicial overreach into policy.","Conclusion: courts have created a rights-based framework; effective action needs clear statutory climate law and accountable institutions."]},{"id":"S2","m":25,"w":450,"q":"The Constitution (130th Amendment) Bill, 2025 proposes removal of a Minister who is detained for thirty consecutive days on a serious charge. Critically examine the proposal in light of the presumption of innocence, federalism and the rule of law.","o":["Proposal: Articles 75, 164 and 239AA amendments; removal after 30 days' detention on offences punishable with 5 years or more (Verify the current status).","Rationale: constitutional morality, public trust, Ministers continuing in office while in custody (Manoj Narula, 2014; Public Interest Foundation, 2018).","Concerns: presumption of innocence and Article 21, detention is not conviction, risk of misuse of agencies against opposition-ruled States, federal balance.","Safeguards suggested: judicial oversight, automatic revival on bail, narrow list of offences, independent review.","Conclusion: the aim is legitimate, but the design must avoid executive misuse and respect basic structure features like federalism and free elections."]}]};
